<main id="main">
  <section class="hero ink">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="hero-icon" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><use href="icons.svg#i-lock"/></svg></span>
      <p class="kicker">Guide</p>
      <h1>Is my customer data sellable?</h1>
      <p class="lead">It can be licensed, but rarely as it sits. What your contracts allow, and how much personal information the records contain, decide the rest.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section mist">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="reveal">Three things decide it</h2>
      <div class="inline-cards">
        <div class="card reveal">
          <span class="icon-tile" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><use href="icons.svg#i-doccheck"/></svg></span>
          <h3>What your contracts say</h3>
          <p>Customer agreements and confidentiality clauses may restrict sharing, require notice, or rule licensing out entirely.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card reveal">
          <span class="icon-tile" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><use href="icons.svg#i-lock"/></svg></span>
          <h3>How much is personal</h3>
          <p>Names, contact details and free-text notes about people all count. Personal information gets its own review, separately from the commercial terms.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card reveal">
          <span class="icon-tile" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><use href="icons.svg#i-layers"/></svg></span>
          <h3>What the tool allows</h3>
          <p>Some platforms limit reuse of content created inside them. That is checked before a dataset is scoped, not after.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section teal-soft">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2 class="reveal">Anonymisation is a standard, not a step</h2>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>Removing names is not enough</strong>Combinations of details can still identify a person, especially in free-text records.</li>
        <li><strong>Free text is the hard part</strong>Notes and messages often carry details that a column-based review never sees.</li>
        <li><strong>Narrowing helps</strong>Reducing the scope to what can be shared safely is usually better than removing the whole dataset.</li>
        <li><strong>Review happens twice</strong>Identifiers are removed or transformed, then the result is reviewed again before delivery.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section mist">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2 class="reveal">What the agreement sets</h2>
      <ol class="path reveal">
        <li><h3>Scope</h3><p>Which records, which years, and which fields are licensed at all.</p></li>
        <li><h3>Purpose</h3><p>What the buyer may use the data for, stated in writing rather than implied.</p></li>
        <li><h3>Length</h3><p>How long the license lasts and what happens at the end of it.</p></li>
      </ol>
      <p class="small-note">You approve the terms before anything is delivered. Your own counsel reviews the agreement; we do not advise on it.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section teal-soft">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2 class="reveal">Common questions</h2>
      <div class="faq reveal">
        <details><summary>Can I sell data my customers created?</summary><p>It depends on your contracts and the tool&rsquo;s terms. The data buyer reviews this before any buyer sees the data.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Can I keep some records out?</summary><p>Yes. Exclusions are normal, and scope is often narrowed to what is clear rather than licensing everything.</p></details>
        <details><summary>What about health or financial details?</summary><p>Some of it is subject to strict rules. Those cases are reviewed individually and may be declined.</p></details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>


  <section class="section mist">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2 class="reveal">What de-identification has to survive</h2>
      <p class="reveal">There are three layers, and most failed attempts stop at the first.</p>
      <ol class="path reveal">
        <li><h3>Direct identifiers</h3><p>Names, email addresses, phone numbers, account numbers. The easy layer, and the only one a find-and-replace can handle.</p></li>
        <li><h3>Quasi-identifiers</h3><p>Job title, employer, city, dates, unusual combinations. Not identifying alone, identifying together.</p></li>
        <li><h3>Free text</h3><p>The hardest layer. A resolution note can describe a person, a company and a dispute in one sentence without naming anyone.</p></li>
      </ol>
      <p class="reveal">The UK Information Commissioner&rsquo;s Office makes the standard concrete: data is anonymised when re-identification is not reasonably likely, considering who could attempt it and with what. Pseudonymised data is still personal data. That is why a column-level review is not the same as a review of the dataset.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section teal-soft">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2 class="reveal">What a customer-data licence usually sets</h2>
      <div class="table-wrap reveal">
        <table class="table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Term</th><th scope="col">The question it answers</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Scope</td><td>Which records, which years, which fields, and which categories are excluded.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Purpose</td><td>What the buyer may do with it. Training, evaluation and benchmarking are different permissions.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Term and exclusivity</td><td>How long the licence runs, and whether you can license the same records to anyone else meanwhile.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Deletion</td><td>When the raw copy is destroyed, and what evidence you get of it.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Audit</td><td>Whether you can inspect a sample before delivery, and on what notice.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Onward transfer</td><td>Whether the buyer may pass the data to another party, and on what terms.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="reveal">Read the fourth row and the second row together. A long exclusivity on a narrow purpose is a very different deal from a short exclusivity on a broad one.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section mist">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2 class="reveal">When the answer is no</h2>
      <p class="reveal">Some records should not be licensed by anyone, and saying so early is cheaper than discovering it in diligence.</p>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>Health information without a consent path</strong>Not impossible, but a separate legal analysis, not a line item.</li>
        <li><strong>Payment card data</strong>Out of scope for the programmes we work with, whatever it is worth.</li>
        <li><strong>Children&rsquo;s data</strong>Also excluded by the buyer&rsquo;s published terms.</li>
        <li><strong>Privileged or under-protective-order material</strong>Belongs to a matter, not to a dataset.</li>
        <li><strong>Client files where the client has not agreed</strong>The professional obligation sits with the firm, and it does not transfer.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="reveal">A refusal here is not a lost deal. It is the reason a seller can say yes to the parts that are clean.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section mist">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2 class="reveal">More questions</h2>
      <div class="faq reveal">
        <details><summary>Can I license the same records to two buyers?</summary><p>Only if the first agreement allows it. Exclusivity is a term you negotiate, and it is usually the most valuable thing you give away.</p></details>
        <details><summary>What if the records are already aggregated?</summary><p>Aggregation lowers risk but does not settle it. Small groups and unusual combinations can still identify, which is why the review looks at the record, not the label.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Do I need to delete anything myself?</summary><p>The buyer&rsquo;s terms usually cover deletion of the raw copy it receives. What stays in your own systems is your decision and your policy.</p></details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

